  "_note": {
    "underscore_keys_are_not_uploaded": "Every top-level key starting with an underscore is documentation for a reader of this file and is stripped before the body is handed to content.add. Otherwise this commentary would end up inside the live web map, where the portal would tolerate it and nobody would want it.",
    "one_service_five_layers": "All five operational layers carry the same url and itemId and differ only in layerDefinition.drawingInfo. One copy of the data, five renderers - that is the architecture of this use case. classBreaks appears twice on purpose: rent_eur_sqm and median_age are classified the same way, and the rent layer exists to be compared against the heatmap drawn from the very same column.",
    "placeholders": "{{FEATURE_SERVICE_URL}} and {{FEATURE_LAYER_ITEM_ID}} are substituted by publish_to_portal.py. A portal item id does not exist until the item is created and changes on every run, so it cannot be committed to output/. An unsubstituted token yields a web map that loads, draws its frame and shows no data, which is why the publisher asserts that no double-brace marker survives before uploading anything. That sentence cannot spell the marker out: the assertion greps the serialised payload as text, so a note quoting the token literally trips the check on its own documentation - which is how this note came to be worded this way.",
    "hand_built": "arcgis 2.4.1.1 has no arcgis.map module and arcgis.mapping no longer exports WebMap, so there is no Web Map class to build this with. content.add with a text body is the only route, not a workaround.",
    "basemap_attribution": "The basemap is somebody else's data and carries their attribution. Never crop the provider notice out of a published screenshot, and record it as text in the README as well (CLAUDE.md section 3)."
  }
